<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cangrande I della Scala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1291">1291</a>–<a href="/years/1329">1329</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cangrande I della Scala was lord of Verona from 1311 to 1329 and the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family. Imperial vicar and Ghibelline leader, he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and Treviso, making Verona the leading power of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He sheltered the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, who dedicated the Paradiso to him, and died days after taking Treviso.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cangrande-i-della-scala.jpg" alt="Cangrande I della Scala" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maestro_dell%27arca_di_mastino_II,_statua_a_cavallo_di_cangrande_della_scala,_1340_ca._03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christened Can Francesco, he was born in Verona on 9 March 1291, the third son of Alberto I della Scala, ruler of Verona, and Verde da Salizzole. His early strength and wit earned him the name Cangrande, big dog, later echoed in the dog's head crest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> lords. He first fought in the campaigns of his brother Alboino I della Scala against Azzo VIII of Este, and in 1308 began to share rule with him and married Giovanna, daughter of Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>; they had no heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Emperor Henry VII made the brothers Imperial Vicars of Verona, and in 1311 Cangrande commanded the Italian levies at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>. He became sole ruler on Alboino's death on 29 November 1311 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> in February 1312, opening a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. On 17 September 1314 he rode from Verona to Vicenza in three hours and routed a Paduan army in its suburbs, and the peace of October 1314 recognised his hold on the city.</p><p>In December 1317 he took Monselice and stormed Este, and in February 1318 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> ceded both, with Castelbaldo and Montagnana. A Ghibelline convention at Soncino named him Captain and Rector of the Imperial Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, and he ignored the excommunication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>. On 25 August 1320 Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> routed his army at Bassanello. Cangrande then won Feltre, Serravalle and Belluno by political means, and on 10 September 1328 he entered Padua as its ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cangrande made his state entry into Treviso on 18 July 1329 but was already ill, according to contemporary accounts after drinking from a polluted spring, and died on 22 July. Tests on his mummified body in 2004 found lethal amounts of digitalis, which points towards poisoning; his successor Mastino II hanged one of his physicians. His tomb over the entrance of Santa Maria Antiqua in Verona was topped by an equestrian statue, now in the Museum of Castelvecchio.</p><p>Of his conquests only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> outlasted Mastino II's reign, but his revision of Verona's statutes lasted to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> was his guest at Verona, generally dated between 1312 and 1318, and praised him in Canto XVII of the Paradiso, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> portrayed him as a wise ruler in the Decameron.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cangrande I della Scala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressure from <b>Cangrande della Scala</b> of Verona led the citizens in 1318 to make Jacopo I da Carrara their lord, beginning the Lordship of Padua.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-padua">Commune of Padua</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cangrande della Scala</b> took Treviso in 1329, joining it to the Lordship of Verona.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-treviso">Commune of Treviso</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1311 <b>Cangrande della Scala</b> took Vicenza, and it became part of the Lordship of Verona.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-vicenza">Commune of Vicenza</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by <b>Cangrande della Scala</b>, the Commune of Padua in 1318 elected Jacopo I da Carrara as its lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-padua">Lordship of Padua</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cangrande I</b>, lord from 1311 to 1329, conquered Vicenza, Padua and Treviso and hosted the exiled Dante, who dedicated the Paradiso to him.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Treviso, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1315" aria-label="Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cangrande I della Scala"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 6177h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 6177h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1315">What was here in 1315? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
